.TH make "" "Command"
.SH NAME
make \- program building discipline
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmake [\-disnpqrst] [\-f \fIfile\fB] [\fImacro\fB=\fIvalue \fB...] [\fItarget ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
make -- program building discipline
Usage:	make [ option ... ] [ argument ... ] [ target ... ]
Options:
	-d	Debug: more than enough output
	-f file	Make instructions are in 'file' (default: [mM]akefile)
	-i	Ignore command error returns
	-n	Test: do all but execute commands
	-p	Print macro definitions and target descriptions
	-q	Only return exit status (zero if files up to date)
	-r	Ignore built-in rules
	-s	Do not print command lines when executed
	-t	Update times of files without regenerating
.HE
.B make
reads a set of dependency rules and shell commands and uses them to
decide which
.I target
files are out of date with respect to the files they depend on, and
to bring them up to date by running the associated commands.
.PP
Arguments of the form
.IB macro = value
appearing before the first
.I target
define
.I macro
for the duration of the run, overriding any definition of the same
name inside the makefile.
.PP
Unless
.B \-r
is given,
.B make
first reads
.B /usr/lib/makemacros
to establish the built-in macros and suffix rules, then reads the
makefile itself, then reads
.B /usr/lib/makeactions
to establish the built-in target actions.
The makefile is read from each
.B \-f
.I file
given, in order; if no
.B \-f
is given,
.B make
looks first for
.B makefile
and then for
.B Makefile
in the current directory.
.PP
If the makefile defines the special target
.B .IGNORE
or
.BR .SILENT ,
.B make
behaves as though
.B \-i
or
.B \-s
respectively had also been given.
If it defines
.BR .DEFAULT ,
the commands attached to that target are used to make any target for
which no other rule applies.
.PP
After the makefile is read,
.B make
brings each named
.I target
up to date, in the order given; with no
.I target
argument, it brings up to date the first target defined in the
makefile.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Print debugging information as
.B make
scans the makefile and decides what to build.
.IP \fB\-f\ \fIfile\fR
Read
.I file
as (part of) the makefile, instead of
.B makefile
or
.BR Makefile .
May be repeated; a
.I file
of
.B \-
means read the makefile from the standard input.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Ignore nonzero exit status from commands run to build a target.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Print the commands that would be executed to bring targets up to
date, but do not execute them.
.IP \fB\-p\fR
Print the macro definitions and the target/dependency/command
descriptions that
.B make
assembled from the makefile, then continue.
.IP \fB\-q\fR
Do not build anything; merely return an exit status of zero if all
named targets are already up to date, nonzero otherwise.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Do not read
.B /usr/lib/makemacros
or
.BR /usr/lib/makeactions ;
rely entirely on rules given in the makefile itself.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Do not print each command line before it is executed.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Update the modification time of each out-of-date target without
actually running its commands.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fBmakefile\fR, \fBMakefile\fR	default input, tried in that order
\fB/usr/lib/makemacros\fR	built-in macro and suffix definitions
\fB/usr/lib/makeactions\fR	built-in target actions
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B make
prints a message and stops if it cannot open a makefile, if a target
has no rule and no file by that name exists, or if a command run to
build a target returns a nonzero exit status (unless
.B \-i
is in effect).
.SH NOTES
Because macro definitions given on the command line override those in
the makefile, they are a convenient way to change, for example, which
compiler or options a makefile uses for a single run.
